### Summary

Three tRPC endpoints in Dokploy's compose router (`compose.templates`, `compose.getTags`, `compose.deployTemplate`) accept a user-controlled `baseUrl` parameter that gets passed directly to server-side `fetch()` calls with no URL validation, no protocol restriction, and no private IP blocking. Any authenticated user can make the Dokploy server send HTTP requests to arbitrary internal or external URLs, allowing access to cloud metadata services (AWS/GCP/Azure), internal network services, and other resources that should not be reachable from the application.

### Details

The three vulnerable endpoints all accept `baseUrl` as `z.string().optional()` with no further validation:

`apps/dokploy/server/api/routers/compose.ts`:

```typescript
// line 687
templates: protectedProcedure
    .input(z.object({ baseUrl: z.string().optional() }))   // no URL validation
    .query(async ({ input }) => {
        const githubTemplates = await fetchTemplatesList(input.baseUrl);
        // ...
    }),

// line 705
getTags: protectedProcedure
    .input(z.object({ baseUrl: z.string().optional() }))   // no URL validation
    .query(async ({ input }) => {
        const githubTemplates = await fetchTemplatesList(input.baseUrl);
        // ...
    }),

// line 574
deployTemplate: protectedProcedure
    .input(z.object({
        environmentId: z.string(),
        serverId: z.string().optional(),
        id: z.string(),
        baseUrl: z.string().optional(),   // no URL validation
    }))
    .mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => {
        const [template, templateLogo] = await Promise.all([
            fetchTemplateFiles(input.id, input.baseUrl),
            fetchTemplateLogo(input.id, input.baseUrl),
        ]);
        // ...
    }),
```

The user-supplied `baseUrl` goes directly into `fetch()` in `packages/server/src/templates/github.ts`:

```typescript
// line 57
export async function fetchTemplatesList(
    baseUrl = "https://templates.dokploy.com"
) {
    const response = await fetch(`${baseUrl}/meta.json`, {   // user-controlled URL
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(10000),
    });
    const templates = await response.json();
    return templates;   // response data returned to caller
}

// line 125
export async function fetchTemplateFiles(templateId, baseUrl = "https://templates.dokploy.com") {
    const [r1, r2] = await Promise.all([
        fetch(`${baseUrl}/blueprints/${templateId}/template.toml`, { ... }),   // user-controlled
        fetch(`${baseUrl}/blueprints/${templateId}/docker-compose.yml`, { ... }),  // user-controlled
    ]);
    // ...
}

// line 88
export async function fetchTemplateLogo(templateId, baseUrl = "https://templates.dokploy.com") {
    const response = await fetch(
        `${baseUrl}/blueprints/${templateId}/${template.logo}`, { ... }   // user-controlled
    );
    // ...
}
```

The fetch URL appends a fixed path like `/meta.json`, but the attacker can neutralize this using a query string. For example, setting `baseUrl` to `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/role-name?x=` makes the full URL `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/role-name?x=/meta.json`. The appended part becomes a harmless query parameter, giving the attacker full control over the effective request path.

There is no URL parsing, no protocol enforcement, no private IP blocklist, no domain allowlist, and no DNS rebinding protection on any of the three endpoints.

